\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Phase conventions in two charged-current matrices}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{\citet{case (A,C), (5)–(6), p. 654, and six-plet and (13), p. 657}{kobayashi1973cp}
consider a charged current with a #{2\times2} unitary mixing
matrix. Rephasing their fields puts it into real rotation
form. Their conclusion about CP in that case assumes the
additional interaction specified there is zero. On p. 657
the larger multiplet gives a #{3\times3} charged-current
matrix whose phases cannot all be removed in general. Their (13)
parametrizes a remaining phase.}

\p{The two operations being compared have different domains.
In \ref{fdip-0191} an arbitrary orthonormal change of basis
may first diagonalize a Hermitian operator on the common
target. The physical rephasing acts on the particular
mixing matrix of the charged current. The finite threshold
in \ref{fdip-0195} concerns the existence of obstructed
Gram pairs in a supplied dimension.}
